00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:37.253
without getting into a fight?

00:01:37.253 --> 00:01:38.684
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; 15 or 20 seconds,

00:01:38.684 --> 00:01:39.881
talk to the people nearby.

00:02:08.936 --> 00:02:09.996
All right, great.

00:02:12.906 --> 00:02:16.187
Good.

00:02:16.187 --> 00:02:19.370
Give a little thank you to
your discussion partner.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.141
Say thank you to your discussion partner.

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:22.660
Great.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:30.240
So, you are all already experts
at what I'm talking about today.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.780
So we're just a room full of experts.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.150
We're talking about power and
the language of power.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.500
It's the secret language we all speak.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.800
Throughout that interaction,
you didn't run into each other.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.620
You didn't fall over each other.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:43.430
You negotiated.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:48.110
Somebody had to decide who gives way and
goes by, and who doesn't.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.771
And there's a lot of smiling, and

00:02:49.771 --> 00:02:52.701
nodding, and head tilting,
that happens all throughout.

00:02:52.701 --> 00:02:55.960
I was watching, not everyone of you,
but I saw a lot of you.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.420
And that's what happening.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.360
That is the secret language of
power that we speak all the time.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:05.530
So, how many of you want power?

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:07.460
Raise your hand nice and
tall if you want power.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:08.010
Oh, yes!

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.570
Yes, I teach in the business school and
I know this.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:13.630
Yeah, we're captains of industry.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:14.144
We want power.

00:03:16.944 --> 00:03:18.390
Not every hand though.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:23.480
How many of you, not really trying to
grasp all that power and amass it?

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:24.591
There's the friendly, nice people.

00:03:24.591 --> 00:03:26.270
I see your smiles, yeah.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.190
So we're kind of split.

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:33.751
I think the most interesting
question is why do we want power?

00:03:33.751 --> 00:03:35.470
Or why do we not want power?

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:38.780
because it has different meanings.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.580
Now, if we had a larger, huge, huge table,

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.730
we could all sit around one big table and
have a nice discussion about it.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:48.640
But since we can't,

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.240
let me pull some discussion
pieces from a few famous people.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.048
Contact with men who wield power and

00:03:56.048 --> 00:03:59.121
authority still leaves
an intangible sense of repulsion.

00:03:59.121 --> 00:04:01.801
It's very much like being in
proximity to fecal matter.

00:04:01.801 --> 00:04:06.770
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now that is an opinion about power

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.570
and that some of you not wanting power
might share some of that sentiment.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.231
But some people have a different opinion.

00:04:15.231 --> 00:04:16.751
I love power.

00:04:16.751 --> 00:04:20.641
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But it is an artist that I love it.

00:04:20.641 --> 00:04:24.216
I love it as a musician loves his violin,

00:04:24.216 --> 00:04:29.030
to draw its sounds and
chords and harmonies.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.320
This guy loves power.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:37.010
He really wants it, but
those are opposing positions.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:42.121
How can it be that we love power and
we hate power.

00:04:42.121 --> 00:04:43.610
Well, why are we averse to it?

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:49.231
For one thing, people do not always
acquire power for the right reasons.

00:04:49.231 --> 00:04:53.570
And right is a perspective, because your
right reason to acquire power might not be

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:57.110
the same as other people, so
there's some discrepancy.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:01.770
But there are definitely people
that pretty universally we can say

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:03.540
try to amass power for the wrong reasons.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:08.799
Because they often misuse it and
it's not pretty.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:11.690
Now we can go back in history and

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:15.810
see some really terrible things that have
happened when people have mass power.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:19.060
But we can look in the paper today and
see that that is still going on.

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:21.590
There is struggle for power,

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:26.000
and there are negative consequences, and
that's part of why we're adverse to it.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.790
We don't want anybody to have too much so
they don't start doing bad things.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:33.900
And we believe in meritocracy, generally.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:34.651
We learn it in school.

00:05:34.651 --> 00:05:37.350
The right answer should be
the thing that prevails.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.230
I mean that's just right, isn't it?

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:44.242
Somewhere deep down we know that little
third grader in all of us is like,

00:05:44.242 --> 00:05:45.811
no that's not right!

00:05:45.811 --> 00:05:48.452
It shouldn't be that way.

00:05:48.452 --> 00:05:50.440
They have the right answer.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:53.350
So we kind of have an aversion to power.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:59.986
But at the same time we also want

00:05:59.986 --> 00:06:05.180
power because we know, we recognize,
there's a time when you need it.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:11.130
Especially when that is in service
of the greater good or someone else.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:14.970
And as a leader of people,
an organization,

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:18.460
a team, a group of children,
whatever it is.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:21.220
There is a time when you
need to wield that power

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:22.760
in a way that helps to serve them.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.040
And I don't think we would disagree
with wanting that kind of power.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.310
Power to keep people safe.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.230
Power to help them achieve what they want.

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:33.560
Those are great uses of power.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:35.561
I think we would all sign up for,
yes, I like power.

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:38.341
I want it in that case.

00:06:38.341 --> 00:06:40.960
And we definitely covet status.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:42.770
We want people to respect us.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:45.270
We do everything we can to
stay off the bottom and

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:48.255
make sure there's at least
that one person that's below.

00:06:48.255 --> 00:06:52.840
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There are videos and things

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:57.440
that show the buckets of crabs, where
they're all clawing each other back down.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:01.750
Not really clawing their way up, but
at least putting somebody below.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:05.390
And sometimes work can feel that
way if it's not a great situation.

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:07.801
But we definitely don't
want to be the bottom.

00:07:07.801 --> 00:07:11.061
And we fight pretty hard.

00:07:11.061 --> 00:07:15.314
And it happens from a very young age,
we start learning it in schools,

00:07:15.314 --> 00:07:17.560
navigating this social hierarchy.

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:22.050
And that's what we're talking about today.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:25.950
That first exercise you did, of get up and
move around, and then have a discussion

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:31.790
about it, is part of a body of work
that is in the class, Acting With Power.

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:35.560
So I'm a lecturer in the organizational
behavior department and

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:40.130
there is an amazing team that I get
to work with that teach this class.

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:43.240
And we have all contributed
the ideas I'm sharing today.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:47.161
What I can tell you is I believe
all of them, and with conviction.

00:07:47.161 --> 00:07:49.960
And I do this work all around the world.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.650
But that class works in
the way that you just did it.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:56.379
We throw the students into an activity,
unexperienced,

00:07:56.379 --> 00:07:59.787
where they're not quite sure why
they're doing it or what's going on.

00:07:59.787 --> 00:08:03.420
It feels a little strange and
then we ask them to reflect on it.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:07.135
And then, we come back and say,
let's put some thought behind it.

00:08:07.135 --> 00:08:11.303
Let's pull some of the research and
try to explain what's going on.

00:08:11.303 --> 00:08:14.235
Now, it's a beautiful
marriage between art and

00:08:14.235 --> 00:08:18.680
science because the science says,
this is why it's happening.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:20.820
But the art side, the doing it and

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:24.670
the experience,
discovers the truths we all know.

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:27.040
And it lets us feel
what's really happening.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:30.630
So that we know it empirically,
and it's nice to know why.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:32.939
So that is what this class
is about that I teach.

00:08:32.939 --> 00:08:36.340
And that is what we're talking about today
and what you are already experts in.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.140
Come on in, find a seat.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.253
Yeah, there's some right here.

00:08:42.253 --> 00:08:43.413
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:08:43.413 --> 00:08:44.214
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:44.214 --> 00:08:45.050
&gt;&gt; No, no it's fine.

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:47.778
It's a great example because they
all know what you're doing now.

00:08:47.778 --> 00:08:51.172
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They all just got up and moved and

00:08:51.172 --> 00:08:51.818
changed seats.

00:08:51.818 --> 00:08:57.227
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Okay, so, why do we have

00:08:57.227 --> 00:09:02.754
this love/hate relationship with power?

00:09:02.754 --> 00:09:06.783
Well, as we all know, when relationships
are challenging, you have to have that,

00:09:06.783 --> 00:09:08.790
define the relationship conversation.

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.560
So let's have a definition
conversation about power.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:13.490
What is power?

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.310
Power means a lot of things to a lot of
people, and we use it in various ways.

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:21.490
So if we're going to actually have
some rigorous discussion about it,

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:22.860
we define it.

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:26.230
You do not have to adopt these, but for
the sake of how we're talking about it

00:09:26.230 --> 00:09:29.615
today, these are how I'm
going to talk about it.

00:09:29.615 --> 00:09:33.120
Power's the capacity to control
resource and administer consequence.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:36.490
Pretty simple.

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:38.180
Resource can be anything.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.690
It could be money, it could be time,
it could be schedule.

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:42.480
It could be responsibility.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:44.440
It could be friendship and social.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:51.390
You have power over someone
when they depend on you.

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:55.749
For things that are important to them,
and in your control.

00:09:55.749 --> 00:09:59.200
Or, things that they cannot
easily get other places.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:03.320
Parents control the candy.

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:08.976
It is something that is important to
the children and in the parents' control.

00:10:08.976 --> 00:10:13.400
And the children, up to a certain age,
cannot get easily on their own.

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:17.926
But there's a balancing equation,

00:10:17.926 --> 00:10:21.376
because children control
the audible space in the house.

00:10:21.376 --> 00:10:24.747
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It is something important

00:10:24.747 --> 00:10:28.322
to the parents and in control of the
children, and that they cannot easily get

00:10:28.322 --> 00:10:31.630
other places, because they
have to stay and tend to them.

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:35.376
So, there is a balance back and
forth that shows up in every relationship.

00:10:38.556 --> 00:10:41.810
Authority, on the other hand,
sounds like power.

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:42.580
It's similar.

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:44.850
It's in the same playing field.

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:48.970
Authority is a characteristic
of a formal role.

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:53.600
So, the right and responsibility to
control, direct, and dominate others.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:58.630
Here is your title, you are the vice
president in charge of this.

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:01.220
You get to control that,
we official mandate that you do,

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:01.940
it's part of your role.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:03.850
It's your formal title.

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:11.340
It is a basis of power, but
it may give you control over resources.

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:18.130
You may have seen a person or
two who had the title but

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:19.800
didn't really control resources.

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:22.200
They're ineffectual.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:23.460
People don't listen to them.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.320
So it's not just authority.

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:30.450
Hopefully, it's not too many of
these people that are out there.

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:31.900
But it does come up.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:33.710
But if that is not the only thing,

00:11:33.710 --> 00:11:38.310
if the title doesn't absolutely give you
the control, there must be something else.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:40.420
And in social science we call it status,

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:45.570
the extent to which others
hold you in high esteem.

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:46.460
Are you respected?

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:50.386
And that is a way you
can control resource.

00:11:51.501 --> 00:11:56.070
It's a condition or a relationship and
it's a basis of power.

00:11:57.450 --> 00:12:02.220
But people on an equal team,
one person can lead that team and

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:05.600
everyone will defer because
of the known expert.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:08.510
Maybe it's the best football player or

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:11.750
the person that knows math
the best in the study group.

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:14.330
And everyone will defer to them.

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:19.700
They suggest that we run a play or
that we study on this set of problems.

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:20.560
And people will listen.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:22.410
They control the resource
of what's happening.

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:28.330
So, power can be derived by authority or
from status.

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:30.760
That seems pretty simple.

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:32.920
So there's our definition
of the relationships there.

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:38.950
One of these is not very
much in your control.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:42.570
It's hard to give yourself a promotion.

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:43.960
If you can do it, I recommend it.

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:46.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Go ahead and do it now.

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:51.570
But, because you usually can't define it,
you need help from other people.

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:56.410
Status is the one that we can play with,
and that's the one totally in our control.

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:59.940
That's how we interoperate and
relate to other people.

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:03.080
So we can get power just
by changing who we are.

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:05.850
And that's kind of a hopeful story,
as far as I'm concerned.

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:11.226
Now, when we talk about power.

00:13:11.226 --> 00:13:17.240
Controlling resources, there's that
love hate thing that comes back in.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:19.590
Whose controlling it, and
what are they using it for?

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:26.696
So, the goal of this work is to
expand the authentic range in this

00:13:26.696 --> 00:13:31.576
equation of how you show up in your
status with power, status, and

00:13:31.576 --> 00:13:36.130
authority, because it's about
improving relationships.

00:13:40.088 --> 00:13:42.280
Communicating better.

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:45.680
And as a leader, and the students
that we have coming through here,

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:48.370
we want them to go out and be effective.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:50.870
And to lead and build good relationships.

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:54.940
Now you can use all of these tools that
we're going to talk about for evil.

00:13:56.710 --> 00:13:58.070
They're just tools.

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:01.260
You can influence other people,
you can amass power and you can use it for

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:02.360
good or evil.

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:06.140
But, our contention is, that if you can

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:10.660
successfully navigate conversations
better, if you can build relationships and

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:14.690
prune them in how you operate with people,
you can change the world.

00:14:14.690 --> 00:14:20.160
You can change organizations and you can
accomplish your leadership goals and

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:23.011
change the way you coach and you mentor
and you facilitate and you lead.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:28.470
That is a worthy goal in understanding
the non-verbal communication and

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:29.090
social hierarchies.

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:32.480
At the end of the day,

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:37.120
it allows you to show up in the way
that your stakeholders need you to.

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:38.120
Your audience.

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:41.130
And your audience is the people
below you in the organization.

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:43.740
The people on the team that
you're trying to bring along.

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:46.480
The non-profit that you work with and
you're on a board.

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:50.440
How can you help to influence them,
build a bond, get respect, and

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:53.550
move things forward for
the goals that you have?

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:58.597
because if your intention is good,
we want to help you move along that route.

00:14:59.618 --> 00:15:04.040
So, power.

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:10.020
I think this encapsulates the whole
idea I was telling you about.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:13.950
The day that the power of love
overrules the love of power,

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:14.910
the world will know peace.

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:18.777
That's a little higher than
we shoot in the class.

00:15:18.777 --> 00:15:22.670
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But not too much higher,

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:25.930
because change lives and
change the world is in the motto.

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:29.280
We really are about how can you,
in your way as a leader,

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:33.200
use love as the basis of leadership,
which sounds a little funny.

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:36.230
But at the end of the day,
it's about people.

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:42.320
And that is a nice principle too,
love can take a lot of forms.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:44.480
Love can be, just in service.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.850
And if you can be in service
of people as a leader, and

00:15:46.850 --> 00:15:50.290
use these tools in that way,
it's a very powerful place to be.

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:58.888
Okay, so, this secret language,
we're talking about, is universal.

00:16:03.396 --> 00:16:05.625
Can you tell which one is on top here?

00:16:05.625 --> 00:16:08.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's very clear.

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:15.006
Does anybody think that this tiger leaning
back is the one that's in control or

00:16:15.006 --> 00:16:15.782
in power?

00:16:15.782 --> 00:16:16.618
Right.

00:16:16.618 --> 00:16:19.776
Plain, 100%, perfect.

00:16:19.776 --> 00:16:20.620
Good.

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:22.490
Next question on the quiz.

00:16:22.490 --> 00:16:23.830
It scales to any size.

00:16:23.830 --> 00:16:28.040
So it doesn't really matter what
size you are, same thing here.

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:28.910
Who has the power?

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:32.900
I'm a dog person so
I like this one a little bit better.

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:36.120
But this is what's going on.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:40.320
There is a whole language that
is being spoken here, and

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:42.310
I see this play out at
the dog park all the time.

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:46.720
There are books on wolf signaling
that explain these things,

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:50.890
and I will go and play with this because
it works across species as well.

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:53.469
And it doesn't matter if you're young or
old.

00:16:54.760 --> 00:16:56.370
That puppy is clearly dominant.

00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:00.555
He's so fierce, or she is.

00:17:02.516 --> 00:17:03.560
But this how we learn it.

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:08.850
Because at a very young we start playing
with these tools with our friends,

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:13.740
with our parents, and
we build these social skills.

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:16.130
It's in the fabric of who we are and
how we operate.

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:18.150
This is how we communicate and

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:22.190
there's no intellectual study
going on in this equation.

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:27.260
This is just learning how do we respond to
the world and this is about relationships.

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:31.190
The interesting thing is that this
also governs our friendships.

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:34.360
They're playing with status,
who's on top and who isn't, but

00:17:34.360 --> 00:17:36.590
that's how you define a friendship.

00:17:36.590 --> 00:17:39.160
Friendships are the ones
where you can play with this.

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:41.170
You can put them down a little bit.

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:45.620
You can call out like there's that story
you know about your best friend that you

00:17:45.620 --> 00:17:48.980
keep trotting out especially
after a glass of wine you're

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:51.450
like oh what about
the incident with the car.

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:53.170
And everybody knows what that is.

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:56.400
It's putting that person down and
saying, yeah you're a really bad driver.

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:57.970
That's really what you're saying.

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:01.280
But it's okay because it's in
this friendly and playful way.

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:08.240
If you have a relationship with someone
where you've known them a long time,

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:11.960
but they never felt like a friend,
I can never hit that level.

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:13.040
There's something different.

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:15.170
It's probably that the status
has never changed.

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:18.430
It's a static condition.

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:19.750
So it scales.

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:21.320
We learned it when we were young.

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:25.160
It goes cross-species.

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:28.270
And not just cross-species there but
cross-species here.

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:30.600
So this is universal stuff
that we're talking about.

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:32.060
It's dominance and submission.

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:38.200
So there is no, oh, that's not how
it works for me, or, I'm from this

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:41.500
region of the country, or this part of the
world, so we don't operate in that way.

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:43.910
This is really basic.

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:46.970
How it shows up has cultural differences,
to be sure.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:51.840
And it's not about age, and
this is just gratuitously cute.

00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:58.460
But, That little girl is the one
who's dominant over that

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:03.960
dog who clearly is a more
powerful animal in that equation.

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:05.990
Could cause harm, but doesn't.

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:07.210
It's giving respect.

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:08.160
That its status.

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:10.719
This is showing an esteem.

00:19:14.144 --> 00:19:15.910
And it pretty much all comes
down to who bites who.

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:21.300
If you are high status or
coming in with that dominance,

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:24.290
you're saying stay away or
I will bite you.

00:19:25.570 --> 00:19:29.510
And if you're submissive you're saying
don't bite me, I'm not worth biting.

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:34.280
It's pretty simple when it
gets down to the basics.

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:36.280
All right.

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:41.340
You can imagine your favorite or
least favorite politician here.

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:44.170
I wanted to stay pretty
neutral on this because

00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:46.310
I have no idea whose who in audience.

00:19:46.310 --> 00:19:49.480
So, you will see it in
politics all the time though.

00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:54.200
We are at a really interesting part
of time where over the next year and

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:55.570
it's already been going on for a year.

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:59.970
But over the next year,
we're going to see the debates and

00:19:59.970 --> 00:20:05.390
a ton of press going on around
the political election.

00:20:05.390 --> 00:20:09.810
And there is so much that happens
in there that you can watch this.

00:20:09.810 --> 00:20:14.760
If somebody does the handshake wrong,
adds the extra arm grab,

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:18.740
doesn't do the handshake or does a toast
where they don't look at each other

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:23.956
right, it's 24 hours on CNN.

00:20:23.956 --> 00:20:27.650
It's 24 hours on CNN because it
means something to all of us.

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:30.820
It's not up there because
we don't understand it.

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:32.670
It's up there because we do understand it.

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:34.670
It says something and
it says something very clear,

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:36.290
and it's this language of power.

00:20:36.290 --> 00:20:37.700
So it's going on all the time.

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:42.050
Okay.

00:20:53.496 --> 00:20:56.666
&gt;&gt; Anybody know what this is?

00:20:56.666 --> 00:20:57.234
&gt;&gt; Someone walking.

00:20:57.234 --> 00:21:02.070
&gt;&gt; Someone walking?

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:03.150
Huh.

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:04.785
Technically it is 16 dots.

00:21:04.785 --> 00:21:13.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But, the 16 dots do come from

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:17.960
a person in a black suit with
lightbulbs on them, that they record.

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:22.020
In fact this is the aggregate
of 300 people doing that.

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:29.510
16 dots of information is a very very
small amount but you all saw a person.

00:21:29.510 --> 00:21:32.420
I've never heard anything
other than that but

00:21:32.420 --> 00:21:37.850
I suppose there could be a dog up
right perhaps, but it's a person.

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:42.260
You're really good at this because
somewhere along the line evolutionary

00:21:42.260 --> 00:21:45.960
biology said you better be
good at recognizing humans and

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:49.680
taking stock of who they are,
are they friend or foe.

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:53.610
So we can recognize it very easily
with not a lot of information.

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:58.130
So just imagine what you get from
all 88 muscles that show up here and

00:21:58.130 --> 00:21:59.420
all that extra information.

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:02.990
Gestures, all of that comes in.

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:09.662
Okay so that's a person, what about that?

00:22:09.662 --> 00:22:16.815
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; A soldier, what was that?

00:22:16.815 --> 00:22:20.090
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; A male, oh a man.

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:23.115
Oh, okay what about this?

00:22:23.115 --> 00:22:27.710
[LAUGH]
No

00:22:27.710 --> 00:22:31.305
one ever laughs at the man,
everyone laughs at the woman.

00:22:31.305 --> 00:22:37.063
[LAUGH] I don't know what that is but

00:22:37.063 --> 00:22:41.242
here they are together.

00:22:45.944 --> 00:22:49.518
They are definitely different.

00:22:49.518 --> 00:22:51.560
What do you notice as
the differences specifically?

00:22:54.060 --> 00:22:56.400
What was that?
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:56.400 --> 00:22:57.770
&gt;&gt; Sorry a little louder.

00:22:57.770 --> 00:23:01.030
&gt;&gt; The hips move on the one on the right,
yeah?

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:01.530
What else?

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:05.160
The feet are closer together on this one?

00:23:06.640 --> 00:23:07.265
The knees are?

00:23:07.265 --> 00:23:10.935
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; What was that?

00:23:10.935 --> 00:23:14.670
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; The head moves less back and

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:16.160
forth on this one.

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:17.485
Oh on that one?

00:23:17.485 --> 00:23:21.311
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; The shoulders and

00:23:21.311 --> 00:23:23.218
arms are more aggressive on this one.

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:30.636
The arms are moving more and
they are moving opposite.

00:23:36.197 --> 00:23:38.232
Interesting, huh?

00:23:38.232 --> 00:23:44.470
So, these are also averages and
then tuning out certain characteristics.

00:23:44.470 --> 00:23:46.800
There is a tool that they have
that you can move some sliders.

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:50.873
And this is moving the male and
female slider to the extremes.

00:23:50.873 --> 00:23:55.900
So some things that you pointed out like
the feet are very together on this one.

00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:58.350
And the arms are a little more in and
the hips move.

00:24:02.753 --> 00:24:05.331
Six and
seven year old girls do not walk this way.

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:08.580
Not in general, and

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:11.410
neither do female athletes going
out to play soccer out here.

00:24:13.300 --> 00:24:14.550
This is learned behavior.

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:21.640
This is something that we adapt to,
for very particular reasons.

00:24:21.640 --> 00:24:24.480
There is a lot of signalling and
cues that go on to this and

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.540
what it says, but
it is a learned behavior.

00:24:26.540 --> 00:24:27.255
It's not innate.

00:24:27.255 --> 00:24:32.190
Turns out, nine and 10-year-old boys

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:36.932
do not have any kind of muscle mass that
requires their arms to be out like this.

00:24:36.932 --> 00:24:38.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:24:38.140 --> 00:24:41.070
that's when they start to move their arms.

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:42.490
They're mimicking.

00:24:42.490 --> 00:24:47.630
So we are being trained to become what
these are, this version of masculine or

00:24:47.630 --> 00:24:48.960
feminine, from a very early age.

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:52.830
I don't think there's anything
intrinsically wrong with that, and

00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:56.920
there's definitely some
biology reasons behind that.

00:24:56.920 --> 00:25:02.005
And you know, the muscles do fill out for
some people, I guess.

00:25:02.005 --> 00:25:06.020
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And in high heels,

00:25:06.020 --> 00:25:07.980
I think this comes up
even a little bit more.

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:09.120
But some other kind of shoes.

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:11.710
When you're out power walking,
it doesn't happen as much.

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:14.590
So it's something that we don't always do.

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:19.400
But it is a certain piece of how we
start to learn this language of power.

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:26.530
What's the right thing for me to be and
how I show up in a different context?

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:30.670
I'm not advocating that it should
be any way, one or the other.

00:25:30.670 --> 00:25:33.500
And there is clearly, in this work,
a couple of things that

00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:37.170
are very deep holes that we can
just go down into for a long time.

00:25:37.170 --> 00:25:41.560
This could all be about gender and we've
got some really good data about gender,

00:25:41.560 --> 00:25:46.090
but I want to keep it just a little bit
up out of that particular one for now.

00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:48.735
I avoided politics I think
rather nicely as well.

00:25:48.735 --> 00:25:51.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So here's different.

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:52.270
This is not male/ female.

00:25:52.270 --> 00:25:54.727
What do you think of this one?

00:25:59.359 --> 00:26:00.043
&gt;&gt; Michael Jackson.

00:26:00.043 --> 00:26:02.625
&gt;&gt; Michael Jackson!

00:26:02.625 --> 00:26:05.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I have never heard that but I love it.

00:26:06.910 --> 00:26:08.020
What else?

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:08.878
I'm going to start that one again.

00:26:08.878 --> 00:26:13.067
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Happy?

00:26:13.067 --> 00:26:14.705
Running.

00:26:14.705 --> 00:26:17.089
Power walker?

00:26:17.089 --> 00:26:19.253
Determined?

00:26:19.253 --> 00:26:21.640
With purpose.

00:26:21.640 --> 00:26:22.415
Okay, what about this one?

00:26:22.415 --> 00:26:29.930
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The right side is always funnier.

00:26:29.930 --> 00:26:31.940
I don't know why.
What about this one?

00:26:33.198 --> 00:26:34.229
&gt;&gt; What was it?

00:26:35.494 --> 00:26:38.441
&gt;&gt; Suave, yeah, suave.

00:26:38.441 --> 00:26:43.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm pretending like

00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:44.235
I know what it means too.

00:26:44.235 --> 00:26:47.959
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah I think I'm not suave.

00:26:51.337 --> 00:26:52.869
They're different yeah?

00:26:52.869 --> 00:26:53.751
&gt;&gt; That person doesn't know.

00:26:53.751 --> 00:26:55.296
&gt;&gt; That one doesn't know how to walk.

00:26:55.296 --> 00:26:58.582
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
[LAUGH]

00:26:58.582 --> 00:26:59.890
&gt;&gt; Yes, yeah.

00:26:59.890 --> 00:27:03.650
They are moving their
knees a little like this.

00:27:03.650 --> 00:27:09.850
But I think that is a function of
the averaging, not any one individual.

00:27:09.850 --> 00:27:11.270
But that is true.

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:17.050
So this one, the slider that gets
moved is nervous and relaxed.

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:24.900
So nervous has energy, so
power walk I think lines up with that.

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:28.530
They didn't have power walk on there,
but they had nervous and energy.

00:27:29.750 --> 00:27:34.110
And then as it turns out,
you can rotate and watch from the side.

00:27:34.110 --> 00:27:37.999
Now during this one,
I am dynamically am moving the sliders.

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:40.550
I'll narrate it.

00:27:40.550 --> 00:27:44.411
This is normal.

00:27:44.411 --> 00:27:49.310
Then I slide to male.

00:27:49.310 --> 00:27:51.726
Then I slide to female.

00:27:51.726 --> 00:27:54.907
Yeah, longer stride.

00:27:54.907 --> 00:28:00.571
You can see the hips swing
from the side as well.

00:28:00.571 --> 00:28:01.443
Interesting huh?

00:28:05.588 --> 00:28:09.426
I have heard more than once that part
of the reason of this in the angle is

00:28:09.426 --> 00:28:10.350
the high heels.

00:28:11.440 --> 00:28:12.590
Is that true?

00:28:12.590 --> 00:28:15.470
Yeah, that is true.

00:28:15.470 --> 00:28:18.570
That bears out in my reasearch
which is informal, but

00:28:18.570 --> 00:28:19.820
almost every audience tells me that.

00:28:21.090 --> 00:28:24.260
So we're very good.

00:28:24.260 --> 00:28:27.870
All of your observations,
accurate for sure.

00:28:27.870 --> 00:28:31.060
It looks like something, and
we're good at recognizing what it is.

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:35.190
This is all that language of power that is
the secret language going on all the time.

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:40.708
Hopefully you're recognizing this and
you're going, oh, yeah, I recognize this.

00:28:40.708 --> 00:28:41.690
This isn't new.

00:28:41.690 --> 00:28:46.370
You go to a cocktail party, circle of
people, and someone approaches and

00:28:46.370 --> 00:28:49.450
the circle usually does one of two things.

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:54.970
It opens up and it lets them in,
or it closes ranks and it doesn't.

00:28:56.610 --> 00:28:57.850
And that is the same thing.

00:28:57.850 --> 00:29:03.080
It's about who we're protecting that
we're going to allow and share our power.

00:29:03.080 --> 00:29:07.090
And just one person in that group
can sort of own the space and

00:29:07.090 --> 00:29:11.020
the power that they have, and
invite them and say, come, join us, and

00:29:11.020 --> 00:29:14.520
bring them into their space and
bend part of the circle.

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:17.090
So we can act individually as agents and

00:29:17.090 --> 00:29:20.310
change this equation and
affect relationships.

00:29:20.310 --> 00:29:22.040
But it's something that's familiar.

00:29:22.040 --> 00:29:26.600
And we know it, we learn it the hard
way in junior high school, primarily.

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:30.600
There's a lot of social dynamic and
we try and experiment and fail.

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:33.970
And it's a lovely, awkward, awful time.

00:29:33.970 --> 00:29:37.800
But it's where we sort out most of
this and then we sort of get set and

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:38.770
we learn our way.

00:29:38.770 --> 00:29:41.300
This is how I navigate the world.

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:43.520
And we're somewhere on this spectrum.

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:49.160
&gt;From this sort of powerful, high place
to this low and more approachable place.

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:57.470
This is the reference for
this is a university in Canada, and so we

00:29:57.470 --> 00:30:01.410
can see male and female; we did heavy and
light, nervous, relaxed, happy, and sad.

00:30:01.410 --> 00:30:03.410
And it's just pulling averaging out.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:08.550
They have a lot more information if you're
interested to go and dig in on that,

00:30:08.550 --> 00:30:09.160
the Biomotion Lab.

00:30:11.140 --> 00:30:14.130
So your turn.

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:14.910
Let's try this.

00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:18.960
Think about the day of the month
that your birthday is on.

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:22.010
For me it's the 7th of July,
so my number is seven.

00:30:22.010 --> 00:30:24.140
Think of what your number of the month is.

00:30:25.630 --> 00:30:31.420
If you are an even numbered day,
you're going to be team blue.

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:33.270
Just remember, I'm team blue right now.

00:30:33.270 --> 00:30:37.860
If you're an odd numbered person,
just remember that you're green.

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:38.670
Blue and green.

00:30:40.090 --> 00:30:42.770
And if you forget,
