00:46:20.900 --> 00:46:22.010
That's the real key.

00:46:22.010 --> 00:46:26.108
We're all all of these, for sure.

00:46:26.108 --> 00:46:30.513
But if you're stuck in one, and
you can't get to the others,

00:46:30.513 --> 00:46:35.345
your choices are limited by your
experience, how you were raised,

00:46:35.345 --> 00:46:39.443
all of those experiences and
how you navigate the world.

00:46:39.443 --> 00:46:42.715
And if you decide I gotta to be
really authoritative today, but

00:46:42.715 --> 00:46:45.335
you can't go there,
it limits your choices.

00:46:45.335 --> 00:46:50.425
So we're trying to expand the range, so
you can go and make the choice you want.

00:46:50.425 --> 00:46:56.853
Because nobody wants to hear,
we're having layoffs, right?

00:46:56.853 --> 00:47:01.688
That persona does not
match with the message.

00:47:01.688 --> 00:47:05.957
Maybe something more like royalty
is going to be better in that case

00:47:05.957 --> 00:47:07.865
where it's more gravitas.

00:47:07.865 --> 00:47:10.989
But there's another one that's general,
and

00:47:10.989 --> 00:47:15.870
I don't want to walk into my niece and
go happy birthday and frighten her.

00:47:15.870 --> 00:47:18.765
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's what matches the message.

00:47:18.765 --> 00:47:20.775
And we all have this wide range.

00:47:20.775 --> 00:47:23.665
But everybody's motivational
speaker is different.

00:47:23.665 --> 00:47:27.905
And everybody's general and rock star,
they're all their version of it.

00:47:30.275 --> 00:47:32.160
We're talking about acting now.

00:47:32.160 --> 00:47:34.350
Because this class is acting with power.

00:47:34.350 --> 00:47:36.870
We've done some of the social science,
but this is the acting part.

00:47:38.190 --> 00:47:40.020
And acting is not about pretending.

00:47:42.180 --> 00:47:44.500
It's a really common misconception.

00:47:44.500 --> 00:47:47.454
Acting is about finding the truth.

00:47:47.454 --> 00:47:51.138
When you see people on television
that are bad actors, or

00:47:51.138 --> 00:47:56.457
in movies that are bad actors, and you can
tell, it's because they're pretending.

00:47:56.457 --> 00:48:00.880
The people who are really amazing
actors are finding the truth.

00:48:00.880 --> 00:48:03.120
And we know from the Stanford
prison experiment and

00:48:03.120 --> 00:48:07.650
other similar things that we've got all
of it in us including the bad stuff.

00:48:07.650 --> 00:48:10.840
And we know from everyday
people who do amazing,

00:48:10.840 --> 00:48:14.480
heroic things,
that we've got the light in us too.

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:16.500
It's just part of the human condition.

00:48:16.500 --> 00:48:19.620
Right circumstances,
we'll all do anything.

00:48:19.620 --> 00:48:21.440
So given that, we can access anything.

00:48:21.440 --> 00:48:25.700
And a really amazing actor can play
an evil horrible person doing terrible

00:48:25.700 --> 00:48:29.120
things, and
then show up as like wonderful father or

00:48:29.120 --> 00:48:32.980
mother in the next one and
they'll both feel very truthful.

00:48:32.980 --> 00:48:36.420
So we're not talking
about being inauthentic,

00:48:36.420 --> 00:48:41.470
about amassing power and being like,
I'm being powerful now, right?

00:48:41.470 --> 00:48:43.090
This is powerful.

00:48:43.090 --> 00:48:45.640
This looks cartoonish, right?

00:48:45.640 --> 00:48:47.580
That's no really powerful.

00:48:47.580 --> 00:48:49.860
Really powerful is, can you be still and

00:48:49.860 --> 00:48:55.280
comfortable in a moment where you're
having that pause that's so effective.

00:48:55.280 --> 00:48:58.490
That's much more powerful than
the puffed up, you know, of that.

00:48:58.490 --> 00:48:59.880
So it's about finding that truth.

00:49:02.880 --> 00:49:05.740
And it's about being fully present and
in the moment right now.

00:49:07.410 --> 00:49:13.230
So mindfulness and yoga are getting
sort of popular in the business realm.

00:49:13.230 --> 00:49:14.210
It's for good reason.

00:49:14.210 --> 00:49:20.130
As the leader, you can show up and deliver
that message with an authentic version of

00:49:20.130 --> 00:49:27.330
how you want to be with the right level
of authoritativeness or approach-ability.

00:49:27.330 --> 00:49:30.100
because you come in too high on authority,
and scare people way, and

00:49:30.100 --> 00:49:33.200
they won't talk to you, and
you don't get the information you need.

00:49:33.200 --> 00:49:37.970
So maybe you need to move a little
over on the more approachable side.

00:49:37.970 --> 00:49:42.890
But if you're always like this and you
get people to do things by being nice and

00:49:42.890 --> 00:49:49.210
they want to do it for you, when it's
that time, you have to stop doing that.

00:49:49.210 --> 00:49:51.900
You need to be able to go
to that range as well.

00:49:51.900 --> 00:49:55.780
You gotta be fully present
in the moment with people.

00:49:55.780 --> 00:49:57.970
And you have to move off of yourself and
focus on them.

00:50:00.390 --> 00:50:02.590
So it ends up being not about you.

00:50:02.590 --> 00:50:06.310
It's about connecting with the other
person and showing up in the way

00:50:06.310 --> 00:50:09.860
they need you to show up to communicate
and build those relationships.

00:50:12.990 --> 00:50:15.210
And it's also about listening and
connecting.

00:50:15.210 --> 00:50:16.750
This is starting to sound good.

00:50:16.750 --> 00:50:20.430
What if everybody that you interacted with

00:50:20.430 --> 00:50:24.170
was their authentic version of
themselves always in the moment.

00:50:24.170 --> 00:50:27.330
They were really fully present and
they listened and connected.

00:50:27.330 --> 00:50:28.630
Like I want to live in that world.

00:50:28.630 --> 00:50:29.980
That's a good set of stuff.

00:50:29.980 --> 00:50:32.160
And that's what acting can offer you.

00:50:33.340 --> 00:50:35.690
So am I saying, go take an acting class?

00:50:37.230 --> 00:50:37.890
Yes.

00:50:37.890 --> 00:50:40.060
I am saying go take an acting class.

00:50:40.060 --> 00:50:41.700
It's a great place, or an improv class.

00:50:41.700 --> 00:50:44.550
That's where I kind of came up,
through improve.

00:50:44.550 --> 00:50:48.840
You can go and play with all of this
stuff and experience it and it's fun, and

00:50:48.840 --> 00:50:49.860
you'll learn something about yourself.

00:50:52.630 --> 00:50:55.190
Because it works outside in and
inside out.

00:50:55.190 --> 00:50:58.390
If you change your body it will
change how you feel inside.

00:50:59.960 --> 00:51:03.080
There's a good Ted Talk by
Amy Cuddy about power poses.

00:51:03.080 --> 00:51:04.400
It's pretty popular.

00:51:04.400 --> 00:51:05.360
People seen that?

00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:06.270
Yeah?

00:51:06.270 --> 00:51:07.150
Yeah.

00:51:07.150 --> 00:51:11.150
Expand your body and
you will feel more powerful.

00:51:12.650 --> 00:51:16.535
Backstage, before I came on,
I was back there doing this.

00:51:16.535 --> 00:51:19.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I had some good music on.

00:51:19.100 --> 00:51:25.360
I have ultimate, epic, movie themes
in Spotify and I listen to that.

00:51:25.360 --> 00:51:27.540
Plug for Spotify I guess.

00:51:27.540 --> 00:51:30.060
But I listen to that and
it makes me feel better.

00:51:30.060 --> 00:51:32.680
I listen to it all the way down,
as I was driving here today, and

00:51:32.680 --> 00:51:36.250
then expanding my body because I wanted
to show up in this way with enough energy

00:51:36.250 --> 00:51:38.955
to muster motivational speaker and
rock star in front of you.

00:51:38.955 --> 00:51:44.000
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But the same thing works the other way.

00:51:44.000 --> 00:51:48.620
When you're feeling something,
it shows up in your body.

00:51:48.620 --> 00:51:54.250
When you did that eye contact experiment,
I saw lots of people shift.

00:51:54.250 --> 00:51:57.600
The first one, they were looking and
looking away and their bodies caved in and

00:51:57.600 --> 00:51:59.280
they were more like this.

00:51:59.280 --> 00:52:03.630
And then they got the other one and
they're like hm.

00:52:03.630 --> 00:52:06.370
Right?
So how you feel shows up in your body so

00:52:06.370 --> 00:52:08.610
you can use that as an indicator.

00:52:08.610 --> 00:52:13.110
If you noticed that you're all folded up,
you know what's going on.

00:52:13.110 --> 00:52:16.900
You can change that by expanding
your body and change how you feel.

00:52:16.900 --> 00:52:21.310
It's sort of like when you're young and
you're a little upset, and

00:52:21.310 --> 00:52:25.510
you go outside and play it's because
you get your body into motion and

00:52:25.510 --> 00:52:27.550
you're emotions change
as a result of that.

00:52:30.210 --> 00:52:32.380
Here's the quick summary
of everything I've said.

00:52:33.480 --> 00:52:36.265
If you want to be more authoritative,
there's five S's.

00:52:36.265 --> 00:52:41.084
Slowness, stillness, sorry, silence,

00:52:41.084 --> 00:52:45.480
space, stillness and symmetry.

00:52:45.480 --> 00:52:47.530
Slowness in how you move and walk.

00:52:49.050 --> 00:52:50.150
Silence.

00:52:50.150 --> 00:52:53.490
We've got pause encompasses that.

00:52:53.490 --> 00:52:56.860
Space is about the space that
you take up and what's yours and

00:52:56.860 --> 00:52:58.380
whether you invade someone else's.

00:53:00.210 --> 00:53:03.610
Stillness is just limiting the motion.

00:53:03.610 --> 00:53:07.030
And symmetry means this, not this.

00:53:08.320 --> 00:53:09.870
This is asymmetric.

00:53:09.870 --> 00:53:13.340
And I would say this is a little bit
less powerful, but more approachable.

00:53:13.340 --> 00:53:17.010
And this is a little bit
more authority and gravitas.

00:53:17.010 --> 00:53:19.500
And maybe a little less approachable.

00:53:19.500 --> 00:53:24.680
So the more you do it,
the less powerful you look.

00:53:26.520 --> 00:53:30.680
And as I start to go a little faster
with this we move into the Fs.

00:53:31.800 --> 00:53:34.800
You're always trying to get your words in,
just.

00:53:36.250 --> 00:53:37.110
So you don't get interrupted.

00:53:39.490 --> 00:53:40.810
Getting your words out fast,

00:53:40.810 --> 00:53:44.220
because you're afraid somebody will
interrupt is a lower status place.

00:53:44.220 --> 00:53:45.400
You have to fight for it.

00:53:46.410 --> 00:53:48.800
If you're powerful and
you're in a place of power,

00:53:48.800 --> 00:53:53.320
you know they're going to stop talking,
and you're going to keep going anyway.

00:53:53.320 --> 00:53:54.680
So, they better stop.

00:53:54.680 --> 00:53:56.410
That's a different-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:53:56.410 --> 00:53:57.220
&gt;&gt; All right,

00:53:57.220 --> 00:53:59.090
that's the other side of this.

00:53:59.090 --> 00:54:04.690
If you have to fill silence, you cannot
stand there to be an empty space.

00:54:04.690 --> 00:54:07.760
That's more on the approachable,
but lower status side.

00:54:07.760 --> 00:54:12.390
Giving space, if anyone can give me a word
that starts with F that means giving

00:54:12.390 --> 00:54:16.160
space, and get out of the way, I would
love it, but for now that F is give space.

00:54:17.760 --> 00:54:22.070
Fidgeting, especially if
you touch your face or

00:54:22.070 --> 00:54:27.800
just those little gestures where anything
you do is a little more low status.

00:54:29.070 --> 00:54:36.310
And folded, that's this, that's sort
of protective, and any kind of folding.

00:54:36.310 --> 00:54:37.360
That's all lower status.

00:54:37.360 --> 00:54:41.680
So it's more approachable, people can and
will walk up to you if you do these.

00:54:41.680 --> 00:54:44.800
And they will stay away if you
do the others a little bit more.

00:54:47.360 --> 00:54:48.840
These work.

00:54:48.840 --> 00:54:53.660
Don't play recklessly late night on public
transit or BART or things with these.

00:54:54.725 --> 00:54:57.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because people do react to this stuff.

00:54:57.600 --> 00:55:01.570
And if somebody is acting tough and
a little crazy, and

00:55:01.570 --> 00:55:05.320
you come on with that authoritative,
it goes to confrontation.

00:55:05.320 --> 00:55:07.310
So be a little careful.

00:55:07.310 --> 00:55:11.220
And this is generally toward
the end of the school year,

00:55:11.220 --> 00:55:14.300
there's a lot of questions about how
should I use this in interviews.

00:55:16.210 --> 00:55:19.810
And it is not be authoritative
put on all these things and

00:55:19.810 --> 00:55:21.595
put on that motivational speaker and
go in.

00:55:21.595 --> 00:55:24.730
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That is not it.

00:55:24.730 --> 00:55:27.180
It is about being comfortable and

00:55:27.180 --> 00:55:31.270
playing with this stuff until it's in
your tool kit and it comes out naturally.

00:55:31.270 --> 00:55:34.180
And then making the strategic
choices of how you want to show up.

00:55:35.350 --> 00:55:39.820
And the baseline rule that I
generally say for interviews for

00:55:39.820 --> 00:55:43.210
them is, match and go just below.

00:55:44.430 --> 00:55:46.493
You're worth talking to,
but not threatening,

00:55:46.493 --> 00:55:47.877
and that's a pretty good thing.

00:55:47.877 --> 00:55:52.766
If you just want to connect with somebody,
just sort of copy them physically and

00:55:52.766 --> 00:55:55.965
vocally and
you'll come in at a pretty even level.

00:55:55.965 --> 00:55:58.209
&gt;&gt; Forfeit Space.

00:55:58.209 --> 00:56:02.732
&gt;&gt; Forfeit Space, winner,

00:56:02.732 --> 00:56:07.053
yeah, thank you, yay.

00:56:07.053 --> 00:56:09.051
I learned something, too.

00:56:09.051 --> 00:56:13.750
I'm going to close with this.

00:56:13.750 --> 00:56:15.660
This is my niece, Danielle.

00:56:15.660 --> 00:56:19.200
She is super fierce, I love her.

00:56:20.480 --> 00:56:25.630
This is her first day body surfing and
she is not going to let go of that board.

00:56:26.720 --> 00:56:31.580
She ends up being a little bit
more on the authoritative side.

00:56:31.580 --> 00:56:36.260
She takes after my sister who is two
years older and I know this really well.

00:56:36.260 --> 00:56:41.360
I see it happening and when I was young,

00:56:41.360 --> 00:56:46.920
I saw my sister as bossy, and
I know that's not the best word anymore.

00:56:46.920 --> 00:56:50.180
What I see now is leadership
skills in my niece.

00:56:50.180 --> 00:56:53.000
And she's good at it,
and I help encourage it.

00:56:54.270 --> 00:56:55.415
The problem is Brady.

00:56:55.415 --> 00:56:59.320
[LAUGH]
And

00:56:59.320 --> 00:57:02.970
he's the same age younger than me and
my sister.

00:57:02.970 --> 00:57:08.570
And this is him trying really hard,
that finger outstretched and

00:57:08.570 --> 00:57:13.600
taking up, it's invading her space,
desperately trying to exert his authority.

00:57:13.600 --> 00:57:16.485
But can you see on his face
that it's not really there?

00:57:16.485 --> 00:57:21.384
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He knows it's coming,

00:57:21.384 --> 00:57:25.430
and so does she,
because look at that expression.

00:57:25.430 --> 00:57:29.630
I can't tell you the muscles that do it,
but I know the expressions is she is about

00:57:29.630 --> 00:57:33.455
to take that away from him, and I watched
it happen because I took this picture.

00:57:33.455 --> 00:57:41.010
[LAUGH] He tries, but he is on the more
approachable side of the world.

00:57:41.010 --> 00:57:42.105
Kind of like his uncle.

00:57:42.105 --> 00:57:45.580
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; One of the things that comes up in

00:57:45.580 --> 00:57:49.840
this work a lot is people say, should I be
more authoritative or more approachable,

00:57:49.840 --> 00:57:53.670
and they tend to think high power, and
that's the way to go, what we want.

00:57:55.040 --> 00:57:55.980
But he's not wrong.

00:57:57.240 --> 00:58:01.690
Brady is just fine and so are you if
you are on the approachable side.

00:58:01.690 --> 00:58:02.820
And so am I.

00:58:02.820 --> 00:58:04.840
It's not that there's a right or a wrong.

00:58:04.840 --> 00:58:06.360
We want the most range.

00:58:06.360 --> 00:58:10.230
And you have found your place,
your comfort zone, and

00:58:10.230 --> 00:58:12.240
that is a great place to be.

00:58:12.240 --> 00:58:13.240
And if you can stretch and

00:58:13.240 --> 00:58:16.620
find few more places that you
could go one way or the other.

00:58:16.620 --> 00:58:19.945
It might help you navigate
a few relationships.

00:58:19.945 --> 00:58:24.626
But everybody is okay on this,

00:58:24.626 --> 00:58:27.705
especially Brady.

00:58:27.705 --> 00:58:29.971
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Do have the next picture,

00:58:29.971 --> 00:58:35.040
where she has- [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I don't, I didn't get that picture.

00:58:36.110 --> 00:58:39.075
But it happens enough I could
go get it this weekend.

00:58:39.075 --> 00:58:43.390
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The thing that I like about this is

00:58:44.420 --> 00:58:46.250
we learn it when we're young.

00:58:46.250 --> 00:58:47.680
They're already experts.

00:58:47.680 --> 00:58:48.730
So we already know it.

00:58:48.730 --> 00:58:51.870
So hopefully I've said that,
and now you know, and

00:58:51.870 --> 00:58:54.460
you will now see this everywhere.

00:58:54.460 --> 00:58:57.249
You have a great opportunity
with all these cocktail parties,

00:58:57.249 --> 00:58:59.153
and things you're going to, and events.

00:58:59.153 --> 00:59:02.460
Just step back for awhile and
watch it all happen.

00:59:02.460 --> 00:59:05.690
People reaching in and invading space and
putting an arm around.

00:59:05.690 --> 00:59:09.060
All this stuff is going on,
so that's one thing.

00:59:09.060 --> 00:59:14.820
The other is there isn't a right or wrong
that both ends of the spectrum are great.

00:59:16.080 --> 00:59:19.928
It's about relationships, it's about
they really do get along very well.

00:59:19.928 --> 00:59:21.182
because they navigate this.

00:59:21.182 --> 00:59:25.200
They're figuring it out and
their going to be okay.

00:59:25.200 --> 00:59:26.568
It's a secret language that we all speak.

00:59:26.568 --> 00:59:27.986
Thank you.

00:59:27.986 --> 00:59:37.986
[APPLAUSE].

00:59:42.458 --> 00:59:44.525
Do we have.

00:59:44.525 --> 00:59:47.560
Okay, so we do have a couple
minutes if you have any questions.

00:59:47.560 --> 00:59:49.120
We have some mics, you can come on up.

00:59:50.160 --> 00:59:51.645
We're going a little into overtime.

00:59:51.645 --> 00:59:58.920
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yes I can, yes I can.

01:00:00.650 --> 01:00:05.680
&gt;&gt; I'd like to ask you what we're
talking about is body language,

01:00:05.680 --> 01:00:10.940
and there are people who feel
body language is more effective.

01:00:10.940 --> 01:00:15.160
I mean, 50,60,
I have one sign that says that says 70%.

01:00:15.160 --> 01:00:19.460
What about all of the communications
that's going on without the bodies.

01:00:20.520 --> 01:00:28.660
The texting, the, you don't pick up any
of the meanings, the feelings, the this.

01:00:28.660 --> 01:00:33.160
&gt;&gt; Yeah, okay, so
with online email, texting,

01:00:33.160 --> 01:00:37.950
that kind of communication and chat,
yeah there's no body language involved.

01:00:37.950 --> 01:00:39.280
We've tried.

01:00:39.280 --> 01:00:41.145
I think it's why we have emoticons.

01:00:41.145 --> 01:00:43.730
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Right, it's trying to insert because

01:00:43.730 --> 01:00:48.930
we're desperate for that other information
to come in so I think we try in that way.

01:00:48.930 --> 01:00:54.499
But it definitely affects
the communication.

01:00:54.499 --> 01:00:58.690
If you've been online at all
&gt;&gt; It's just a lot of arguing and

01:00:58.690 --> 01:01:02.120
positional stuff and
I think it doesn't happen when you put two

01:01:02.120 --> 01:01:06.420
actual people in a room because
there is a more fluid communication.

01:01:06.420 --> 01:01:09.870
I think the relationships in person
are better than they are online.

01:01:11.750 --> 01:01:16.160
&gt;&gt; I have grandchildren who feel if
you're not texting, then that's it.

01:01:16.160 --> 01:01:17.140
You text.

01:01:17.140 --> 01:01:18.290
Don't bother to call.

01:01:19.360 --> 01:01:20.740
That's old fashioned.

01:01:20.740 --> 01:01:23.681
Don't say I want me to sit down and talk.

01:01:23.681 --> 01:01:24.940
It's texting.

01:01:24.940 --> 01:01:26.760
&gt;&gt; Yes, I know.

01:01:26.760 --> 01:01:28.700
&gt;&gt; So we are losing something.

01:01:28.700 --> 01:01:30.350
&gt;&gt; I think we are losing something.

01:01:30.350 --> 01:01:31.664
I think we are.

01:01:31.664 --> 01:01:34.439
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

01:01:34.439 --> 01:01:35.460
&gt;&gt; Great, yeah?

01:01:35.460 --> 01:01:38.424
&gt;&gt; Class of '65.

01:01:38.424 --> 01:01:39.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah!

01:01:39.470 --> 01:01:42.360
&gt;&gt; Would you talk about
power at the species level?

01:01:42.360 --> 01:01:45.520
How is it that our species
has got this notion

01:01:45.520 --> 01:01:47.770
that we are the most
powerful on the planet.

01:01:47.770 --> 01:01:50.290
By golly we deserve it, and
we can get away with whatever we want.

01:01:50.290 --> 01:01:52.245
Is that a problem, and
how do we deal with that?

01:01:52.245 --> 01:01:57.842
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah,
we can applaud that, yeah.

01:01:57.842 --> 01:02:00.270
I think, demonstrably, it is a problem.

01:02:00.270 --> 01:02:02.360
There are plenty of problems
that are caused by that.

01:02:03.560 --> 01:02:08.850
And I think it's part of why we are averse
to power, is because we as a species tend

01:02:08.850 --> 01:02:14.890
to consume Take and
use up all of the resource.

01:02:16.810 --> 01:02:20.270
I don't know what we can
do specifically about it.

01:02:21.820 --> 01:02:25.666
I think from this work,
hopefully relationships and

01:02:25.666 --> 01:02:29.690
building better relationships
is one of the keys to it.

01:02:29.690 --> 01:02:32.082
Because we have to work together.

01:02:32.082 --> 01:02:35.542
We're quickly growing to the point
where there's not much left,

01:02:35.542 --> 01:02:38.200
and we're going to have to work together.

01:02:38.200 --> 01:02:41.030
And I think this is one key to it.

01:02:41.030 --> 01:02:45.390
I go all over the world teaching and
working with this, and

01:02:45.390 --> 01:02:47.710
it's the same, it's universal.

01:02:47.710 --> 01:02:50.500
The space, we talk about space and
invading space.

01:02:50.500 --> 01:02:52.350
It's different in different cultures.

01:02:52.350 --> 01:02:57.270
How much space is okay to be
between you is different.

01:02:57.270 --> 01:02:59.150
But what's universal is,

01:02:59.150 --> 01:03:03.550
if I cross that line whatever
distance it is, that's dominant.

01:03:03.550 --> 01:03:05.110
So there are some universal things and

01:03:05.110 --> 01:03:07.580
I think it can help
improve our communication.

01:03:07.580 --> 01:03:13.610
But I wish I had a save
the world message for you, yes.

01:03:13.610 --> 01:03:14.283
&gt;&gt; Richmond, you're talking about.

01:03:14.283 --> 01:03:15.004
&gt;&gt; Hi.

01:03:15.004 --> 01:03:15.584
&gt;&gt; How are you doing?

01:03:15.584 --> 01:03:18.532
When you're talking about authoritative,

01:03:18.532 --> 01:03:22.550
you mentioned taking up space and
also being silent.

01:03:22.550 --> 01:03:27.225
And I'm wondering about,
there's an apparent tension

01:03:27.225 --> 01:03:30.335
that sometimes people who
are authoritative take up vocal space.

01:03:30.335 --> 01:03:30.861
&gt;&gt; Oh, yes.
&gt;&gt; And

01:03:30.861 --> 01:03:34.275
can you say a little bit more
about how those two work together?

01:03:34.275 --> 01:03:37.255
&gt;&gt; Yes, I mean,
space works physically and vocally.

01:03:38.645 --> 01:03:41.545
People that dominate a conversation and

01:03:41.545 --> 01:03:44.925
don't leave room,
that's an act of dominance and power.

01:03:46.630 --> 01:03:50.338
I was just watching
Bill Maher's television show.

01:03:50.338 --> 01:03:53.152
And there was woman sat
between two men and

01:03:53.152 --> 01:03:58.557
the one on the left was incredibly
dominating, interrupted her so many times.

01:03:58.557 --> 01:04:00.322
And she did some beautiful moves.

01:04:00.322 --> 01:04:04.037
In the middle of doing
it to help affect that.

01:04:04.037 --> 01:04:08.924
One, he started talking and she just put
her hand out and rested it on his arm and

01:04:08.924 --> 01:04:12.180
left it there, until she stopped talking.

01:04:12.180 --> 01:04:12.990
And it shut up him up.

01:04:12.990 --> 01:04:16.380
It was very subtle but it was dominating.

01:04:16.380 --> 01:04:18.335
It was just like that cat paw on there.

01:04:18.335 --> 01:04:21.770
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; To do that.

01:04:21.770 --> 01:04:26.220
And the second one is she started talking,
and he interrupted and she said, oh,

01:04:26.220 --> 01:04:27.125
you know where I'm going?

01:04:27.125 --> 01:04:30.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And then let him go.

01:04:30.630 --> 01:04:34.764
And she kind of claimed the vocal
space that this is my idea, but

01:04:34.764 --> 01:04:36.173
I'll let you say it.

01:04:36.173 --> 01:04:39.883
And so I thought those were really,
really evolved and

01:04:39.883 --> 01:04:44.088
nice ways of dealing with that
situation because it comes up a lot.

01:04:44.088 --> 01:04:48.104
Yeah, yeah, I'm going to go over here and
then I'll come back, yeah.

01:04:48.104 --> 01:04:50.101
&gt;&gt; I know you didn't wan to
get into the gender stuff.

01:04:50.101 --> 01:04:50.936
&gt;&gt; Yes?

01:04:50.936 --> 01:04:55.078
&gt;&gt; But I have to ask if you
have any thoughts, insights,

01:04:55.078 --> 01:05:00.898
interesting research about the negative
consequences of power in women.

01:05:00.898 --> 01:05:04.335
&gt;&gt; Yes, look at me distance myself.

01:05:04.335 --> 01:05:09.536
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Wow, that's what I mean.

01:05:09.536 --> 01:05:12.183
You can watch your own body language and
realize what's going on.

01:05:12.183 --> 01:05:15.131
Okay, let me step into this.

01:05:15.131 --> 01:05:20.422
So I get to, I'm lucky enough to teach
with Professor Deb Rumfeld here,

01:05:20.422 --> 01:05:24.693
and she has some fantastic
research about gender in this.

01:05:24.693 --> 01:05:27.710
She's working on a book so
there's going to be more soon.

01:05:27.710 --> 01:05:31.821
One of the things is that there is
a warmth competence gap for women.

01:05:31.821 --> 01:05:35.995
That the perception of men and
the perception of women, on men,

01:05:35.995 --> 01:05:39.800
is that the warmer they are,
the more competent they are.

01:05:39.800 --> 01:05:43.100
And the more competent they are,
the warmer they are.

01:05:43.100 --> 01:05:48.010
The perception of both men and women on
women, is that the more warm they are,

01:05:48.010 --> 01:05:52.220
the less competent, and
the more competent, the less warm.

01:05:52.220 --> 01:05:55.685
And that is not a great
piece of data that we have.

01:05:55.685 --> 01:06:00.399
But it is a true piece of data that's
observational and not prescriptive.

01:06:00.399 --> 01:06:07.449
But, and I think this is not
news to the women in the room.

01:06:07.449 --> 01:06:08.493
Have you experienced this?

01:06:08.493 --> 01:06:09.740
Yeah, yeah.

01:06:09.740 --> 01:06:13.500
So, what can we do about it?

01:06:13.500 --> 01:06:15.820
Society has to change for
that gap to close.

01:06:15.820 --> 01:06:20.866
But with this, having the widest
authentic range, and being able to

01:06:20.866 --> 01:06:26.090
be fluid across it and make choices,
I think is the best way of doing it.

01:06:26.090 --> 01:06:29.190
It turns out it's the best thing for
men, as well.

01:06:29.190 --> 01:06:34.967
But that is the most effective
is to be dynamic in that.

01:06:34.967 --> 01:06:39.527
The trap that came up,
sort of in the 80s and

01:06:39.527 --> 01:06:44.815
90s, was thinking that you
have to make a choice.

01:06:44.815 --> 01:06:48.015
And it's like,
well I'm going to make my career path, and

01:06:48.015 --> 01:06:51.430
I'm going to be competent, and
I don't care what they think.

01:06:51.430 --> 01:06:54.736
And that's a valid choice, and
you know, you can go with that, but

01:06:54.736 --> 01:06:57.292
I think it doesn't have to be so
one end or the other.

01:06:57.292 --> 01:07:00.752
So that's a piece of it,
one of the ones we have the most data on.

01:07:00.752 --> 01:07:02.494
Yeah, you're welcome.

01:07:02.494 --> 01:07:06.789
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Okay, great.

01:07:06.789 --> 01:07:10.332
&gt;&gt; Okay, so I'm thinking that
it's good to be fluid and

01:07:10.332 --> 01:07:13.186
to be able to move in different spectrums.

01:07:13.186 --> 01:07:17.357
But if we find ourselves, let's say
at low status, low warmth, weak and

01:07:17.357 --> 01:07:21.035
repellent, is there a I'm okay,
you're okay, is that okay?

01:07:21.035 --> 01:07:26.350
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They're all okay.

01:07:27.520 --> 01:07:31.730
It's what is the relationship and
how you want to cross and

01:07:31.730 --> 01:07:33.820
does it match with what your saying.

01:07:33.820 --> 01:07:37.190
Where I think this shows up is
sometimes we're just unaware of

01:07:37.190 --> 01:07:39.700
where we're showing up or
how we are showing up.

01:07:39.700 --> 01:07:46.660
And it can be as simple as you walk in,
a bad commute and not feeling that great.

01:07:46.660 --> 01:07:49.470
And you can be sort of down, low and cold.

01:07:50.670 --> 01:07:51.810
And that's what you're putting off.

01:07:51.810 --> 01:07:56.170
And if you know that that's a possibility,
then when you hit the door, you can say,

01:07:56.170 --> 01:07:57.160
is that how I want to show up?

01:07:58.250 --> 01:07:59.820
And, it's okay to go, yup.

01:07:59.820 --> 01:08:02.160
I feel terrible, and stay away.

01:08:02.160 --> 01:08:03.290
And, go ahead and do that.

01:08:03.290 --> 01:08:08.990
But, you can also stop at the door and
go, okay, it's an important meeting.

01:08:10.890 --> 01:08:15.300
I'm going to put this on now, and
by putting it on I'll start to feel it and

01:08:15.300 --> 01:08:16.810
go and have that moment.

01:08:16.810 --> 01:08:18.140
So, making choices.

01:08:19.440 --> 01:08:20.540
This is a little generalized.

01:08:20.540 --> 01:08:23.390
There's a large, nuanced conversation.

01:08:23.390 --> 01:08:27.206
The main idea is that there's
authoritative and approachable, but

01:08:27.206 --> 01:08:29.257
there's also warm and cold on both.

01:08:29.257 --> 01:08:31.440
So yeah, thank you.

01:08:31.440 --> 01:08:33.275
Yes, last one.

01:08:33.275 --> 01:08:34.528
Yeah.

01:08:34.528 --> 01:08:41.903
&gt;&gt; Yes, can you take Roosevelt, Churchill,
Reagan, Bill Clinton, and tell them, and

01:08:41.903 --> 01:08:47.380
tell us where you would place them in the-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

01:08:47.380 --> 01:08:49.490
&gt;&gt; That is a great question and

01:08:49.490 --> 01:08:53.285
actually that is what we do with this in
our classes, we get from the students

01:08:53.285 --> 01:08:57.688
who's in these quadrants and go through
them and those names frequently come up.

01:08:57.688 --> 01:09:03.250
Oprah comes up,
up in that top one as well, often.

01:09:03.250 --> 01:09:07.740
So I would be guessing on a few of those

01:09:07.740 --> 01:09:11.490
because I'm an organizational behavior
lecturer and not a history one.

01:09:11.490 --> 01:09:13.040
So I don't know the depth of them.

01:09:13.040 --> 01:09:19.050
But powerful leaders tend
to be on the higher side,

01:09:19.050 --> 01:09:21.440
and then they vary from warm to cold.

01:09:23.550 --> 01:09:26.495
I would say Bill Clinton
is on the warm side.

01:09:26.495 --> 01:09:31.510
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

01:09:31.510 --> 01:09:34.105
Vladimir Putin is on the colder side.

01:09:34.105 --> 01:09:38.020
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So there is a range.

01:09:38.020 --> 01:09:40.170
Both leaders, both powerful.

01:09:40.170 --> 01:09:42.340
Both got things done.

01:09:42.340 --> 01:09:47.367
So, that is another piece of this,
is, again, neither one is right.

01:09:47.367 --> 01:09:51.784
You can be a leader and be authoritative
and cold, and you can be a leader and

01:09:51.784 --> 01:09:53.836
be more warm, and they both work.

01:09:53.836 --> 01:10:00.694
&gt;&gt; Speaking style, royalty,
you know, motivational speaker,

01:10:00.694 --> 01:10:06.922
in that grouping any easy
placement of those presidents?

01:10:06.922 --> 01:10:13.538
&gt;&gt; Generally, high status and
I would say, varied on warmth.

01:10:13.538 --> 01:10:18.386
Churchill's a little before me, but my,
the images and things that I have seen,

01:10:18.386 --> 01:10:20.430
seems a little cold.

01:10:20.430 --> 01:10:23.669
Seems a little on the cooler side,
and more stoic.

01:10:23.669 --> 01:10:28.195
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; It

01:10:28.195 --> 01:10:32.237
might be more royalty, yeah, yeah, great.

01:10:32.237 --> 01:10:33.421
Thank you.

01:10:33.421 --> 01:10:34.559
&gt;&gt; Great.
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Emily.

01:10:34.559 --> 01:10:41.740
[MUSIC]

